UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 12, Lesson 2: Making a map of a place', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"To make a map, plan the area, choose features, decide symbols and a key, add a compass and scale, and give it a title. A grid of squares helps place features accurately, and a review asks what the map shows well and what it leaves out.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nChoose symbols for a tree, a tap, a bench and a handball court. Why should each symbol be different?\n\nFEEDBACK\nFor example: green circle, blue square, brown rectangle and a grey rectangle with lines. Different symbols let the reader tell features apart using the key.\n\nWATCH FOR\nUsing the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nDraw a grid map of the school ground or a room with ten features. Include a key, compass, scale and title. Write one sentence on what the map shows well and one on what it leaves out.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"To make a map, plan the area, choose features, decide symbols and a key, add a compass and scale, and give it a title. A grid of squares helps place features accurately, and a review asks what the map shows well and what it leaves out.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and respond","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-GEO-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Draw a grid map of the school ground or a room with ten features. Include a key, compass, scale and title. Write one sentence on what the map shows well and one on what it leaves out. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using plan on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read.. Correction: For example: green circle, blue square, brown rectangle and a grey rectangle with lines.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating plan on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with plan, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about plan with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for plan. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for plan. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["For example: green circle, blue square, brown rectangle and a grey rectangle with lines","Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Choose symbols for a tree, a tap, a bench and a handball court. Why should each symbol be different?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for plan; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["To make a map, plan the area, choose features, decide symbols and a key, add a compass and scale, and give it a title","For example: green circle, blue square, brown rectangle and a grey rectangle with lines","Careful use of grid and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named grid and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply plan to new material and name the grid and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read","Name grid and explain why it matters only, without linking it to plan"],"question":"Which response best applies plan with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence appropriately for this lesson on plan. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read","For example: green circle, blue square, brown rectangle and a grey rectangle with lines","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail"],"question":"Which response best uses sources, places or historical/geographic evidence for plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read","Name a specific source or place detail and say what it shows about plan"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (To make a map, plan the area, choose features, decide symbols and a) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or place detail","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail","Example plan: a playground map on a grid of ten squares by eight squares, with one square equal to five paces","Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check facts, acknowledge limits of the evidence, and avoid inventing details","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster"],"question":"Which action keeps sources, places or historical/geographic evidence accurate for plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example","Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read","Name grid and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Draw a grid map of the school ground or a room with ten features"],"question":"Which independent response best applies plan to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about plan with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating plan on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using plan on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for plan. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with plan, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read.. Correction: For example: green circle, blue square, brown rectangle and a grey rectangle with lines.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-hsie-y3-w12-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA HSIE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (HS2-*); content-point mapping and local First Nations protocols remain pending human review.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise plan.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Geography","success_criteria":["I can use plan with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify grid and explain why it matters.","I can practise scale independently on a new prompt.","I can notice title needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"To make a map, plan the area, choose features, decide symbols and a key, add a compass and scale, and give it a title.","options":["To make a map, plan the area, choose features, decide symbols and a key, add a compass and scale, and give it a title","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample plan: a playground map on a grid of ten squares by eight squares, with one square equal to five paces. Symbols: a green circle for a tree, a blue square for a tap and a brown rectangle for a bench. Title, key and north arrow are added last.\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose symbols for a tree, a tap, a bench and a handball court. Why should each symbol be different?\n\nFEEDBACK\nFor example: green circle, blue square, brown rectangle and a grey rectangle with lines. Different symbols let the reader tell features apart using the key.","explain":"To make a map, plan the area, choose features, decide symbols and a key, add a compass and scale, and give it a title. A grid of squares helps place features accurately, and a review asks what the map shows well and what it leaves out.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Grid and scale for a playground map.","caption":"Plan before drawing.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Grid</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">10 by 8 squares</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Scale</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">1 square = 5 paces</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Example plan: a playground map on a grid of ten squares by eight squares, with one square equal to five paces. Symbols:  Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Example plan: a playground map on a grid of ten squares by eight squares, with one square equal to five paces. Symbols:  Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample plan: a playground map on a grid of ten squares by eight squares, with one square equal to five paces. Symbols: a green circle for a tree, a blue square for a tap and a brown rectangle for a bench. Title, key and north arrow are added last. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Symbols and finishing touches.","caption":"Every feature gets its own symbol.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Symbols</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">green circle tree, blue square tap</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Last</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">title, key, north arrow</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nUsing the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read. Here is a more productive model: Example plan: a playground map on a grid of ten squares by eight squares, with one square equal to five paces. Symbols: a green circle for a tree, a blue square for a tap and a brown rectangle for a bench. Title, key and north arrow are added last.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nFor example: green circle, blue square, brown rectangle and a grey rectangle with lines. Different symbols let the reader tell features apart using the key.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Using the same symbol for several features, or leaving off the key, makes the map hard to read. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use the same HSIE idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTo make a map, plan the area, choose features, decide symbols and a key, add a compass and scale, and give it a title. A grid of squares helps place features accurately, and a review asks what the map shows well and what it leaves out. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same HSIE idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","ex